{
  "version": 1,
  "what": "Self-healing's platform pack, as data, per config dialect (docs/HEAL_TO_INTENT.md section 5 and N13; read by modules/heal/kinds.py). Two declarations. SECURITY: the security-control classes by line shape (C2839, the operator, 2026-11-09): a unit that removes or changes a line of one is Tier 2 (a proposal: a person removes it, back to intent, or keeps it and adds it to intent), the ACL class's kind being security_acl and every other class's security_control; adding back a missing one stays what it was. Each class names its line shapes (`lines`, Python regular expressions searched in the line as the device shows it, leading spaces removed) and the section headers every line under which is the class (`sections`). REFERENCES: the definitions a line references by name (C2822, the operator, 2026-11-08, approved as proposed): each object's defining lines (`defines`, a global line, the name in the group `name`) and the lines that use it (`uses`, any section, the names in the group `names`, separated by spaces); an added definition that only one added unit of the same departure references is one unit with it (N13's dependents), and a definition an unchanged line also uses stays its own unit.",
  "how": "Shapes written from the IOS and IOS-XE command references for each feature and checked against the lab's captured configurations (tests/fixtures), never keyed on a device or a name. IOS-XE takes IOS's declarations (`same_as`): these lines are one grammar on both, as the parsers and removal.SHAPES read them. A dialect with no entry has no security classes declared, and self-healing then treats every removal or change on it as a person's (kinds.py says so by name); it is never read with another dialect's unless its entry says `same_as`.",
  "dialects": {
    "cisco_ios": {
      "security": {
        "routing_auth": {
          "words": "routing authentication or a key chain",
